ndividual says 'in Box B I am almost certainly to lose' — this is a misstatement, as 45% is not 'almost certain'. This suggests a misunderstanding of the probabilities. Despite this, the individual is implicitly comparing the minimum probabilities (loss probabilities) and choosing the one with lower (i.e., higher) minimum probability of loss — which is B. Thus, the individual is considering the minimum probability of loss and seems to justify the choice based on a lower minimum probability of loss (45% vs. 90%).",
